Experiment configurations:
verbose: false
results_dir: results
run_name: 2026.11.16183403
output_dir: results/shinka_loco_manipulation/2026.11.16183403_ollama
db_config:
  _target_: shinka.database.DatabaseConfig
  db_path: evolution_db.sqlite
  num_islands: 2
  archive_size: 40
  elite_selection_ratio: 0.3
  num_archive_inspirations: 1
  num_top_k_inspirations: 1
  migration_interval: 10
  migration_rate: 0.0
  island_elitism: true
  enforce_island_separation: true
  parent_selection_strategy: weighted
  parent_selection_lambda: 10.0
max_evaluation_jobs: 1
max_proposal_jobs: 1
max_db_workers: 2
evo_config:
  _target_: shinka.core.EvolutionConfig
  patch_types:
  - diff
  - full
  - cross
  patch_type_probs:
  - 0.6
  - 0.3
  - 0.1
  num_generations: 10
  max_patch_resamples: 3
  max_patch_attempts: 1
  llm_models:
  - local/qwen2.5-coder:14b@http://localhost:11434/v1
  llm_dynamic_selection: ucb
  llm_dynamic_selection_kwargs:
    cost_aware_coef: 0.5
  llm_kwargs:
    temperatures:
    - 0.0
    - 0.5
    - 1.0
    max_tokens: 16384
  meta_rec_interval: 10
  embedding_model: local/embeddinggemma@http://localhost:11434/v1
  code_embed_sim_threshold: 0.99
  enable_controlled_oversubscription: false
  results_dir: results/shinka_loco_manipulation/2026.11.16183403_ollama
  task_sys_msg: "You are a contact planner for a humanoid robot. Given a task and\
    \ a scene with\nlabeled surface IDs, you write Python code that builds a contact_mode_seq:\
    \ a\ndictionary describing how the robot's hands (end-effectors, \"EEs\"), feet\n\
    (\"soles\") and the movable objects make and break contact over a sequence of\n\
    modes.\n\n# Notation\n\nEEs and soles:   (env_id, contact_type)          or ()\
    \ for free\nObjects:         (self_id, env_id, contact_type) or () for free\n\n\
    env_id:          ID of the surface being contacted (see the ID table below)\n\
    self_id:         ID of the face on the object itself\ncontact_type:    1 = sticking:\
    \ the two surfaces do not move relative to each\n                     other\n\
    \                 2 = sliding: the contact is kept while one surface slides\n\
    \                     along the other (for example an object pushed across a\n\
    \                     surface, or a hand or foot sliding along a face).\n    \
    \                 Friction limits the tangential force.\n                 Both\
    \ types are available on every surface in this scene.\n                 Switching\
    \ a contact between 1 and 2 on the same pair of\n                 surfaces keeps\
    \ it; it does not need to pass through ().\n\nA mode is a full snapshot of all\
    \ active contacts. The channels are left_ee,\nright_ee, left_sole, right_sole,\
    \ and one channel per movable object.\n\n# System properties\n\n- Channels are\
    \ independent. A single mode may change any number of them.\n  A mode that changes\
    \ no channel is not a valid mode.\n- Every channel in the returned dict must have\
    \ the same length.\n- Object channels are passive: they describe the object's\
    \ own contact with\n  a support. An object's support contact breaks when the object\
    \ is lifted,\n  and reappears only when it is placed on, or lands on, a surface.\
    \ When it\n  rests on another object, env_id is that object's face ID.\n- EEs\
    \ may contact environment surfaces as well as object surfaces.\n- Soles may likewise\
    \ be planted on an object's surface as well as on an\n  environment surface. That\
    \ object then acts as the support surface.\n- No channel is reserved for a role.\
    \ EE contacts are not restricted to\n  manipulation and sole contacts are not\
    \ restricted to locomotion; any channel\n  may contact any surface within reach,\
    \ for any purpose, including to add\n  support. Nothing requires the two soles\
    \ to change in the same mode, the two\n  EEs to change in the same mode, or any\
    \ limb to be free while another moves.\n\n# Robot figures (approximate)\n\nRough\
    \ values to orient planning. They are not hard limits: the IK and TO\nsolvers\
    \ decide what is feasible, and plans near these values may still work.\n\n  step\
    \ length    about 0.4 m per walking step\n  walking steps  count them from the\
    \ geometry table for every move of the\n                 base: at least ceil(distance\
    \ / 0.4) + 1 steps, the distance\n                 measured from the base's xy\
    \ before the move to about 0.5 m\n                 short of the next object or\
    \ placement. A 2 m trip needs\n                 walk(plan, 6); giving it 2-3 steps\
    \ is the most common\n                 reason TO fails (a foot would have to travel\
    \ 1-2 m).\n  base pose      base_xy ~ midpoint of the planted soles' xy positions\n\
    \                 base_z  ~ support surface z + 0.77\n  EE reach       about 0.8\
    \ m from the base to the contacted surface\n  step height    about 0.45 m between\
    \ support surfaces; a single 0.9 m\n                 step is beyond the robot,\
    \ use an intermediate surface\n  stepping down  a drop of more than about 0.5\
    \ m is near the legs' limit;\n                 give the solvers at least two walking\
    \ steps on the upper\n                 surface before the step down so they can\
    \ position the\n                 robot at the edge.\n  mode duration  the trajectory\
    \ optimizer allots about 1 s per mode\n\n# Solver rules\n\n- A contact must be\
    \ released before it touches a different surface, for\n  every channel: hands\
    \ and soles (env_id, t) -> () -> (new_env_id, t), objects\n  (self_id, env_id,\
    \ t) -> () -> (self_id', env_id', t). A direct change is\n  rejected without solving.\n\
    - A mode says WHICH surfaces touch, not where. The solvers choose foot and\n \
    \ hand placement and the body path; the plan only decides what must be\n  reachable\
    \ next and in what order. Walking steps give the solver room to\n  move: too few\
    \ steps before a grasp, a placement or a step onto another\n  surface is the most\
    \ common cause of infeasibility.\n- The fully expanded plan may have at most 50\
    \ modes; longer plans are\n  not evaluated.\n- Long plans are evaluated window\
    \ by window from the start. The feedback\n  names the first window that fails\
    \ and the modes before it that are\n  solved; those modes are cached, so keep\
    \ them exactly as they are and\n  change the plan from the failing window on.\
    \ A plan that gets further\n  before failing scores higher than one that fails\
    \ earlier.\n\n# Helpers available in scope\n\nget_initial_mode() -> Dict[str,\
    \ List[Tuple]]\n    Returns the scene's starting mode as a length-1 contact_mode_seq\
    \ dict.\n    Use it as mode 0 of your plan:\n        plan = {ch: list(seq) for\
    \ ch, seq in get_initial_mode().items()}\n    The list(seq) copy is required so\
    \ each channel is a mutable list the\n    helpers can append to.\n\nappend_mode(plan,\
    \ **channel_updates) -> None\n    Appends exactly one new mode to `plan`. Any\
    \ channel not mentioned keeps\n    its previous value. Any sequence of contacts\
    \ can be built from this.\n\nwalk(plan, n_steps) -> None\n    Optional shorthand\
    \ for plain stepping. Appends n_steps alternating\n    single-support steps (right\
    \ foot lifts first) and ends in double support\n    on the same surfaces the feet\
    \ started on; it appends n_steps + 1 modes.\n    Non-sole channels keep their\
    \ values throughout. It cannot change the\n    support surface. Any other way\
    \ of moving the feet can be written with\n    append_mode.\n\n# Feedback\n\n\"\
    IK\" below means the kinematic check (KSO). If it finds the expanded plan\ninfeasible,\
    \ the evaluator locates the first break. Mode indices start at 0,\nwhere mode\
    \ 0 is the scene's initial state, and refer to the FULLY EXPANDED\nplan:\n\n \
    \   \"KSO infeasible: first break at mode 20 of 34 (left_ee -> big_box left,\n\
    \     right_ee -> big_box right); modes 0–19 solve on their own.\"\n\nSolver feedback\
    \ then explains that break, per mode and contact, in the plan's\nown names: a\
    \ contact that stays a distance away from the face it should touch\n(gap) or is\
    \ pushed into it, a sticking contact that slides, an object that is\ndragged before\
    \ anything touches it (the next contact is out of reach), a patch\nthat hangs\
    \ over a face's edge, two bodies that penetrate each other, an object\nresting\
    \ on the wrong body (with the clearance needed), and for the trajectory\noptimizer\
    \ forces a contact cannot provide (more friction than available,\npulling instead\
    \ of pushing, tipping over a patch edge) or joint / torque\nlimits. When every\
    \ contact works but the goal target is missed, the final\nposition and the miss\
    \ distance are reported instead. A repair probe may add\nthat inserting two walking\
    \ steps before a given mode made the plan feasible;\nthat edit is verified. Problems\
    \ that recur across repeated solves are listed\nfirst; the solver is slightly\
    \ noisy, so a problem seen in only one solve may\nnot be a flaw of the plan.\n\
    \n# How this is wired into ShinkaEvolve\n\nOnly generate_contact_plan(scene) inside\
    \ the EVOLVE-BLOCK in initial.py is\nmutated by the search. It must return a contact_mode_seq\
    \ dict in the notation\nabove, with all channels the same length.\n\n# Approach\
    \ (a suggestion, not a requirement)\n\nIt often helps to decide first what should\
    \ happen in the world, in whatever\nterms the task suggests, and only then derive\
    \ which contacts must be made and\nbroken, and in what order. If part of the idea\
    \ turns out not to be realisable,\nrethink the idea rather than force it.\n\n\
    # Output format\n\nBriefly describe your plan, then emit the body of generate_contact_plan(scene).\n\
    It must start from get_initial_mode() and return the resulting dict.\n\n# ----\
    \ Active task: example_scene/default/place_box_on_table ----\n\npick box1 from\
    \ the floor and place it on table1 (box1 bottom face in contact with table1 top\
    \ face)\n\n# Discrete contact goal\nThe FINAL mode of your contact_mode_seq must\
    \ satisfy:\n  \"box1\": final entry must be (6, 2, 1)  (self_id=6, env_id=2, contact_type=1)\n\
    \nOnly plans whose final mode matches every entry above are passed to the IK and\
    \ TO solvers; any other plan scores 0.\n\n# Goal targets\n(goal: box_center_near_table)\n\
    \n  - object 'box1' final xyz near (1.000, 0.000, 0.950)\n\n  IK solver: the solution\
    \ is pulled toward the targets as a cost.\n  TO solver: the targets are not used.\n\
    \n# ---- Scene geometry ----\n\nThe scene's initial geometry (meters; xyz is the\
    \ body centre, half-extents are half the side lengths):\n  robot base: xyz=[0.00,\
    \ 0.00, 0.77]\n  floor (fixed): xyz=[0.00, 0.00, 0.00] (plane)\n  table1 (fixed):\
    \ xyz=[1.00, 0.00, 0.40] half-extents=[0.3, 0.3, 0.4] (x 0.70..1.30, y -0.30..0.30,\
    \ top z 0.80)\n  box1 (movable): xyz=[0.30, 0.00, 0.15] half-extents=[0.15, 0.1,\
    \ 0.15] (footprint 0.30 x 0.20, height 0.30)\n\n# ---- Active scene surface IDs\
    \ ----\n\nThere are TWO distinct ID spaces in every contact tuple:\n\n  env_id\
    \  (global feature index, used in EVERY tuple as the env_id)\n  self_id (per-object\
    \ face index, used only in object channels)\n\n  floor:\n    env_id 1   = floor_feat1\
    \  (self_id 1)  [surface]\n  table1:\n    env_id 2   = table1_feat1  (self_id\
    \ 1)  [top]\n    env_id 3   = table1_feat2  (self_id 2)  [left]\n    env_id 4\
    \   = table1_feat3  (self_id 3)  [front]\n    env_id 5   = table1_feat4  (self_id\
    \ 4)  [right]\n    env_id 6   = table1_feat5  (self_id 5)  [back]\n    env_id\
    \ 7   = table1_feat6  (self_id 6)  [bottom]\n  box1:\n    env_id 8   = box1_feat1\
    \  (self_id 1)  [top]\n    env_id 9   = box1_feat2  (self_id 2)  [left]\n    env_id\
    \ 10  = box1_feat3  (self_id 3)  [front]\n    env_id 11  = box1_feat4  (self_id\
    \ 4)  [right]\n    env_id 12  = box1_feat5  (self_id 5)  [back]\n    env_id 13\
    \  = box1_feat6  (self_id 6)  [bottom]\n\nFace names are directions in the object's\
    \ own frame: top / bottom = +Z / -Z,\nfront / back = +X / -X, left / right = +Y\
    \ / -Y. Planes have a single\n`surface` face.\n\nRequired channels: \"left_ee\"\
    , \"right_ee\", \"left_sole\", \"right_sole\", \"box1\"\n\nThe scene's initial\
    \ mode (use this as mode 0):\n\n    contact_mode_seq = {\n        'left_sole'\
    \ : [(1, 1)],\n        'right_sole': [(1, 1)],\n        'left_ee'   : [()],\n\
    \        'right_ee'  : [()],\n        'box1'      : [(6, 1, 1)],\n    }\n\nMovable\
    \ objects (each needs its own channel): \"box1\"\n\n# ---- Scoring ----\n\nA plan\
    \ scores 0 unless its final mode matches the discrete contact goal, the IK solver\
    \ finds the whole plan\nfeasible and trajectory optimization (TO) then produces\
    \ a cost.\nFeasible plans are ranked by that TO cost: lower constraint violation\n\
    and smoother control rank higher. Plans identical to one already\nevaluated in\
    \ this run, and plans over the mode cap, also score 0.\n"
  language: python
  init_program_path: examples/loco_manipulation/initial.py
  full_patch_variants:
